        "target": "Given the problem, I first identified the key quantities and the unknown:\n- Bobby's salary: $450 per week\n- Federal taxes: 1/3 of $450\n- State taxes: 8% of $450\n- Health insurance: $50 per week\n- Life insurance: $20 per week\n- Parking fee: $10 per week\n- The unknown: the final amount left in Bobby's paycheck\n\nTo solve this, I needed to calculate the total deductions and subtract them from Bobby's salary. I broke this down into steps:\n1. Calculate federal taxes: \\( \\frac{1}{3} \\times 450 = 150 \\) dollars\n2. Calculate state taxes: \\( 0.08 \\times 450 = 36 \\) dollars\n3. Sum the fixed deductions: health insurance ($50), life insurance ($20), and parking fee ($10)\n4. Add all deductions together: \\( 150 + 36 + 50 + 20 + 10 = 266 \\) dollars\n5. Subtract total deductions from salary: \\( 450 - 266 = 184 \\) dollars\n\nI expected the result to be the final amount left in Bobby's paycheck, which I verified by ensuring all calculations were correct and the subtraction was accurate. The concrete check I made was to recompute the total deductions and ensure they summed to $266, and then subtracting this from $450 to get $184.\n\nThe operation I would reuse is the step-by-step calculation of total deductions and then subtracting them from the salary. This method is reliable and can be applied to similar problems involving multiple deductions from a fixed amount.\n\nFINAL: 184",
