          {
            "content": "Given: The private company raised $2500 and donated 80% of it to a public foundation. This foundation then distributes the donation equally among 8 organizations.\n\nFirst, calculate the amount donated to the public foundation:\n\\[ 80\\% \\text{ of } \\$2500 = 0.80 \\times 2500 = \\$2000 \\]\n\nNext, distribute this amount equally among the 8 organizations:\n\\[ \\frac{\\$2000}{8} = \\$250 \\]\n\nEach organization will receive $250.\n\nFINAL: 250",
            "role": "assistant"
          },
          {
            "content": "Write your own reusable record of this solved task.",
            "role": "user"
          }
        ],
        "student_prefix_sha256": "d65ab054a03521717e36d004bbb4f5de6bd369db419dce44fbe17958a554152f",
        "target": "Given the problem, I identified the key quantities: the total amount raised by the private company, which is $2500, and the percentage donated to the public foundation, which is 80%. The unknown quantity is the amount each of the 8 organizations will receive. \n\nFirst, I calculated the amount donated to the public foundation by finding 80% of $2500. The operation I used was:\n\\[ 0.80 \\times 2500 = 2000 \\]\nThis calculation was straightforward and directly related to the given percentage and total amount. I expected the result to be a whole number, which it was, confirming the arithmetic was correct.\n\nNext, I needed to distribute the $2000 equally among the 8 organizations. The operation for this was:\n\\[ \\frac{2000}{8} = 250 \\]\nThis division operation was chosen because it evenly splits the total donation among the organizations. I expected each organization to receive a whole number, which $250 is, ensuring the distribution was fair and accurate.\n\nTo check the result, I performed the reverse operation: multiplying the amount each organization received by the number of organizations and comparing it to the total donation to the foundation:\n\\[ 250 \\times 8 = 2000 \\]\nThis check confirmed that the distribution was correct, as the total matches the amount donated to the foundation.\n\nThe operations I used are reusable in similar problems involving percentage calculations and equal distribution. I would apply the same steps when faced with a similar scenario, ensuring to first calculate the total amount to be distributed and then divide it evenly among the recipients.\n\nFINAL: 250",
